WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.059 --> 00:00:02.100
The VHS look has been popular lately

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.380
in music videos and other video art,
as the 90s come back into fashion:

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.495
all neon colours and diagonal lines

00:00:07.495 --> 00:00:09.912
and over-enthusiastic voices!

00:00:11.938 --> 00:00:13.771
I cannot keep that up.

00:00:15.625 --> 00:00:17.530
Now, some producers will
actually take the time

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.816
to get their nice, crisp HD footage
and put it through

00:00:19.816 --> 00:00:22.129
an actual video recorder
to get that effect,

00:00:22.129 --> 00:00:24.369
but let's be honest, most won't.

00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:27.394
Most will use the same filter
as everyone else, so I figured

00:00:27.394 --> 00:00:28.815
to explain how it works

00:00:28.815 --> 00:00:31.505
I would talk to the guy
who wrote that filter.

00:00:31.505 --> 00:00:36.138
- VHS has very muted colour
because more information

00:00:36.138 --> 00:00:38.924
is dedicated to the
black-and-white information

00:00:38.924 --> 00:00:41.640
or the luminance part of the image.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.584
The colour suffers a little bit,
so it gets downsampled,

00:00:44.584 --> 00:00:45.871
there's less information there,

00:00:45.871 --> 00:00:49.556
it tends to kinda get softened
and bleed outside of the edges and stuff.

00:00:49.556 --> 00:00:51.750
When we originally gathered
footage, so,

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:55.947
they went out to Goodwill and
second-hand stores and picked up

00:00:55.947 --> 00:01:00.265
as many VHS and SVHS decks
that they could find.

00:01:00.265 --> 00:01:03.299
With the decks and camcorders that we had,

00:01:03.299 --> 00:01:07.393
we used a clean reference image
that we could later

00:01:07.393 --> 00:01:10.617
compare the recorded
version on the deck to.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:15.060
So, when you take an original, clean
version and the post-VHS version

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.458
and line them on top of each other,

00:01:17.458 --> 00:01:18.738
you can create what we
call a lookup table,

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:23.060
which allows us to define the colour
differences between the two images.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:26.060
But a lot of the time,
people are quite young,

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.440
applying these retro filters or styles,

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:32.840
and have sort of an idea of what they're
supposed to look like,

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:36.178
so it's a little bit of both.

00:01:36.178 --> 00:01:39.038
- VHS is an analog format,
it's not storing ones and zeros,

00:01:39.038 --> 00:01:41.115
it's storing patterns on a magnetic tape,

00:01:41.115 --> 00:01:43.510
and that means that
copies aren't bit-perfect.

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:45.101
Not only is there random noise,

00:01:45.101 --> 00:01:48.090
but there's also some
interesting analog glitches.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.555
- We start with a blank tape,
it looks like this.

00:01:50.555 --> 00:01:53.523
When you throw it in to
one of the VCR decks,

00:01:53.523 --> 00:01:55.280
one of them has
the lid off of it,

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:01.200
and that is the one that actually
died making the VHS transitions.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.420
That one went through a lot

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.171
and all of that got
printed onto this tape.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:13.740
Essentially just taking the device
and then doing a lot of bad things to it.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.660
While the tape is loaded in
through the playback heads,

00:02:16.665 --> 00:02:18.825
you can run a magnet across them

00:02:18.825 --> 00:02:21.882
and just really mess up how
it's going to play that back,

00:02:21.882 --> 00:02:25.196
and tracking be damned:
it'll flutter and be all over the place.

00:02:25.196 --> 00:02:28.794
This one, I believe, died
based on a belt failure,

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:34.080
but that belt failed while I
was using two pencil erasers

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.610
that were taped to my fingers
slowing down the head

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.100
while dropping a magnet on
top of it.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.470
It was interesting,

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:46.885
and then all of a sudden it
just mechanically stopped.

00:02:46.885 --> 00:02:48.821
The hardest part about that
was just getting the tape out.

00:02:48.821 --> 00:02:51.123
- There is a section in the code

00:02:51.123 --> 00:02:53.345
that is just called 'tape damage'.

00:02:53.345 --> 00:02:56.205
There are things like what
we call the pop lines,

00:02:56.205 --> 00:02:59.213
when you are recording tape
and you get pieces of dust

00:02:59.213 --> 00:03:01.285
or debris or whatever stuck to the tape

00:03:01.285 --> 00:03:03.138
and it rolls across the scan head,

00:03:03.138 --> 00:03:06.865
that one line will create
a streak across the screen,

00:03:06.865 --> 00:03:09.375
and then there's also a scrolling wrinkle.

00:03:09.375 --> 00:03:11.364
Generally it's repeating
cause often that happens when

00:03:11.364 --> 00:03:13.748
the tape jams up or something like that,

00:03:13.748 --> 00:03:15.197
and you end up with a bunch of wrinkles

00:03:15.197 --> 00:03:17.208
that somebody then
reassembled the tape back.

00:03:17.208 --> 00:03:20.223
- Of course, there are some things
that a filter can't add, like:

00:03:20.223 --> 00:03:25.080
Camera angles! Neon typography!
And fake enthusiasm!

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:28.980
But perhaps some things are
better left in the past.

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:34.460
- "And there's more from Tom
at the same time next week.

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:38.400
"Coming up later, Captain Disillusion
is going around in circles,

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.700
"but next, a programme chosen for you
by the Algorithm."

